/** /docs/llms.txt. */
export const DOCS_LLMS_TXT = "# Insulin Apps\n\n> Open-source business apps hosted at insulinapps.com: one account and organization for every app, roles from Owner to Viewer, AI agents over MCP and service accounts for each app's API.\n\nThese pages explain how to set up an organization, sign in, manage people and roles, connect AI agents and call the API, and how to use each app. Each link is the page's Markdown; the same page as HTML is at the address without .md. @@ORIGIN@@/docs/llms-full.txt has every page in one file.\n\n## Platform\n\n- [Getting started](@@ORIGIN@@/docs/platform/getting-started.md): Create an Insulin Apps account, set up your organization, invite your team and open your first app.\n- [Organizations and roles](@@ORIGIN@@/docs/platform/organizations-and-roles.md): Organization roles, access to specific apps, custom roles, and the pages where owners and admins manage people.\n- [Sign-in and security](@@ORIGIN@@/docs/platform/sign-in-and-security.md): Passwords, Google, two-step sign-in, enterprise single sign-on with SAML or OIDC, and SCIM provisioning.\n- [AI agents over MCP](@@ORIGIN@@/docs/platform/ai-agents.md): Connect Claude Code, Codex, Gemini CLI, opencode or a hosted connector to an app's MCP server, and what an agent may do there.\n- [API access with OAuth clients](@@ORIGIN@@/docs/platform/api-and-service-accounts.md): Create an OAuth client on an app's API access tab, get a client-credentials token, rotate its secret without downtime, and call the app's API, starting with GET /api/catalog.\n- [Integrations](@@ORIGIN@@/docs/platform/integrations.md): An app's integrations page with its stored credentials for outside services, and the platform services apps use beyond their database.\n- [How it works](@@ORIGIN@@/docs/platform/how-it-works.md): The architecture of Insulin Apps: the gateway, the app Workers and their databases, the identity headers and the platform services.\n- [FAQ](@@ORIGIN@@/docs/platform/faq.md): Short answers to the questions people ask most about accounts, organizations, roles, AI agents and data on Insulin Apps.\n- [Glossary](@@ORIGIN@@/docs/platform/glossary.md): Plain definitions of the terms used across Insulin Apps, from access token to service account and SCIM.\n\n## Cap Table\n\n- [Cap Table overview](@@ORIGIN@@/docs/captable/overview.md): What Cap Table does, who it is for, what it leaves to other tools, and how it runs on Insulin Apps.\n- [Setting up Cap Table](@@ORIGIN@@/docs/captable/setting-up.md): Create your company in Cap Table, either empty or by importing Pulley's reports or the Cap Table spreadsheet template.\n- [Everyday tasks](@@ORIGIN@@/docs/captable/everyday-tasks.md): Step by step, how owners, admins and editors keep the cap table up to date once the company is set up.\n- [Roles and access](@@ORIGIN@@/docs/captable/roles-and-access.md): What Admins, Editors and Viewers can do in Cap Table, how custom roles narrow it, and what holders see.\n- [Cap Table API](@@ORIGIN@@/docs/captable/api.md): Cap Table's HTTP API for AI agents and OAuth clients, every endpoint with the access it needs, and how changes are made safely.\n- [Equity basics](@@ORIGIN@@/docs/captable/equity-basics.md): The ideas behind a cap table and employee equity in plain words, with what Cap Table does about each. General information, not advice.\n- [How Cap Table works](@@ORIGIN@@/docs/captable/how-it-works.md): The append-only ledger behind Cap Table, how the cap table is replayed for any date, how corrections work, and how data stays separate.\n- [Cap Table glossary](@@ORIGIN@@/docs/captable/glossary.md): The terms used in Cap Table and in these docs, from 409A valuation to warrant, in plain words. General information, not advice.\n\n## Esign\n\n- [Esign overview](@@ORIGIN@@/docs/esign/overview.md): What Esign does, who it is for, its pages, and how it runs on Insulin Apps. Experimental, for synthetic documents only.\n- [Getting started with Esign](@@ORIGIN@@/docs/esign/getting-started.md): Open Esign, upload your first template, send an envelope and follow it to a signed PDF with its certificate.\n- [Templates and fields](@@ORIGIN@@/docs/esign/templates.md): Upload a PDF with the roles that sign it, signed on an added page or in signature, title and date fields you place in the editor.\n- [Sending envelopes](@@ORIGIN@@/docs/esign/sending-envelopes.md): Send a template to one signer per role, set a sign-by day, follow statuses, void an envelope and let due work finish the rest.\n- [Signing](@@ORIGIN@@/docs/esign/signing.md): What a signer sees and does on Esign's signing page, from the one-time link and the emailed code to signing, declining and their copy.\n- [Roles and access](@@ORIGIN@@/docs/esign/roles-and-access.md): What each role can do in Esign, its two permissions and custom roles, how signers get access, and how AI agents and OAuth clients reach it.\n- [Esign API](@@ORIGIN@@/docs/esign/api.md): Esign's management API for host products, OAuth clients and AI agents, its public signer endpoints, and how to embed the signing page.\n- [How Esign works](@@ORIGIN@@/docs/esign/how-it-works.md): The signing lifecycle, the hash chain, codes and sessions, the KMS and RFC 3161 seal, the certificate of completion, what is stored, and known limits.\n- [Electronic signatures in general](@@ORIGIN@@/docs/esign/e-signatures.md): Background on electronic signatures, what usually makes one hold up, and where Esign stands. General information, not legal advice.\n- [Esign glossary](@@ORIGIN@@/docs/esign/glossary.md): The terms Esign uses, from audit trail to void, in plain words.\n"
